 update public.study_materials set content=$pilot$<h2>Hardware, Logic Gates and Boolean Algebra</h2>
<p><strong>Cambridge International AS &amp; A Level Computer Science 9618 (2026):</strong> this note follows syllabus statements 3.1, 3.2 and 15.2. It covers computer components and systems, gate functions and representations, Boolean algebra, adders, SR/JK flip-flops and Karnaugh maps. CPU architecture and the fetch-execute cycle are treated separately under statement 4.1.</p>
<h3>1. Computer systems and embedded devices (3.1)</h3>
<p>A computer system receives data, processes it, stores data or instructions, and produces output. Input devices convert user actions or measured physical quantities into usable data. A microphone converts sound into an electrical signal; a touchscreen both detects touch input and displays visual output. Output devices present processed results: speakers convert electrical signals into sound, while a laser printer forms a page image and fixes toner to paper. A 3D printer builds an object layer by layer from a digital model. A virtual-reality headset presents a close stereoscopic display and may sense head movement so the scene can respond.</p>
<p>Secondary storage keeps programs and files when power is removed. A magnetic hard disk stores data magnetically on rotating platters and uses moving heads; it offers high capacity but is vulnerable to mechanical shock. Solid-state flash storage stores charge in memory cells and has no moving parts, so it is fast and shock-resistant, though capacity costs more and cells have finite write endurance. An optical reader/writer uses a laser to read or record a disc. Primary memory is directly available to the processor while programs run; it is not a substitute for persistent secondary storage.</p>
<p>An embedded system is a computer built into a larger product to perform a specific or limited set of functions, often for monitoring or control. Benefits include compact size, low power use, reliable repeated operation and lower cost than a general-purpose computer. Limitations include less flexibility, difficulty upgrading or repairing it, and the risk that a failure affects the whole device. Suitability depends on the product and the consequences of failure.</p>
<h3>2. Primary memory and buffers (3.1)</h3>
<p>RAM is volatile read/write primary memory: its contents are lost when power is removed. It holds programs and data currently in use. ROM is non-volatile and holds instructions that must remain available, such as start-up firmware. Do not confuse ROM with secondary storage: ROM is primary memory with a persistent purpose.</p>
<ul><li><strong>PROM</strong> can be programmed once after manufacture; it cannot then be erased and rewritten.</li><li><strong>EPROM</strong> can be erased using ultraviolet light and programmed again; conventionally the chip must be removed from its circuit for erasure.</li><li><strong>EEPROM</strong> can be erased and reprogrammed electrically, often while installed. Flash memory is a widely used form of electrically erasable non-volatile memory.</li></ul>
<p>SRAM stores each bit using a bistable circuit and does not need periodic refresh while power is present. It is fast but uses more circuit area per bit, so it is comparatively expensive and lower-density; it is used where speed matters, such as cache. DRAM stores each bit as charge in a capacitor. Charge leaks, so it must be refreshed periodically. DRAM is slower than SRAM but simpler per bit, cheaper and higher-density, making it suitable for main memory. Both SRAM and DRAM are volatile.</p>
<p>A buffer is a temporary memory area holding data during transfer between devices or processes that operate at different rates or transfer different-sized blocks. A printer buffer queues output while the printer works more slowly than the processor can send data. Buffering smooths transfer and lets the sending process continue; it is not permanent storage and does not itself make the receiving device faster.</p>
<h3>3. Monitoring, control, sensors and feedback (3.1)</h3>
<p>A monitoring system samples sensor readings and records, displays or reports them. It observes a process but does not automatically change it. A control system uses sensor input, compares the measured state with a required condition, and can send an output to an actuator. A temperature sensor may measure a greenhouse; a heater is an actuator. Pressure, infrared and sound sensors are further examples named in the syllabus.</p>
<p>In a feedback control loop, the system measures the result of its action and uses that new measurement to decide what to do next. For a thermostat, the target temperature is compared with the measured temperature: if the room is too cool, the controller switches the heater on; once the target is reached, it switches it off. Feedback helps the controller respond to changing conditions. A one-way system that acts without measuring the result is open-loop, not feedback control.</p>
<h3>4. Logic gates and representations (3.2)</h3>
<p>A logic gate maps binary input value(s) to a binary output. A NOT gate has one input; the other gates in this syllabus have two inputs. AND is 1 only when both inputs are 1; OR is 1 when at least one input is 1; XOR (EOR) is 1 when exactly one input is 1, so it is 0 when inputs match. NAND is NOT(AND), and NOR is NOT(OR). The prime mark means NOT and parentheses show which operation is complemented.</p>
<table border="1" cellpadding="6"><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>NOT A</th><th>AND</th><th>OR</th><th>NAND</th><th>NOR</th><th>XOR</th></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></table>
<p>The same logic can be represented as a problem, Boolean expression, circuit diagram or truth table. Translate carefully: identify inputs, write the operation and parentheses, then evaluate every input combination. For Y=(A OR B) AND NOT C, evaluate the OR and NOT sub-expressions before the final AND. A circuit must preserve this grouping; a truth table must include all 2ⁿ rows for n inputs and show the output for each row.</p>
<h3>5. Boolean algebra and De Morgan's laws (15.2)</h3>
<p>Boolean algebra works with values 0 and 1. Here A·B means A AND B, A+B means A OR B, and A' means NOT A. These symbols are not ordinary arithmetic: 1+1=1 because 1 OR 1 is 1. Useful identities include A+0=A, A·1=A, A+A=A, A·A=A, A+A'=1 and A·A'=0. Use identities to simplify a circuit without changing its output for any input.</p>
<p>De Morgan's laws are (A·B)' = A' + B' and (A+B)' = A'·B'. To complement a bracketed expression, complement each input and change AND to OR or OR to AND. Example: (A·(B+C))' = A' + (B'·C'). Check the scope of the complement: A·B' is not the same as (A·B)'. Absorption identities include A+A·B=A and A·(A+B)=A. A truth-table check can confirm a proposed simplification.</p>
<h3>6. Adders: half adder and full adder (15.2)</h3>
<p>A half adder adds two one-bit inputs A and B. Its Sum output is S=A XOR B; its Carry output is C=A AND B. Thus 0+0 gives S=0,C=0; 0+1 and 1+0 give S=1,C=0; 1+1 gives S=0,C=1. The carry is passed into the next binary column. A half adder has no carry-in input.</p>
<p>A full adder also accepts carry-in Cin. Sum is 1 when an odd number of A, B and Cin are 1: S=A XOR B XOR Cin. Carry-out is 1 when at least two of the three inputs are 1: Cout=(A·B)+(A·Cin)+(B·Cin). For example, 1+1+1 has binary total 3, so S=1 and Cout=1. Keep Sum and Carry as separate outputs; exchanging their gate functions is a common error.</p>
<h3>7. Flip-flops and data storage (15.2)</h3>
<p>A flip-flop is a bistable circuit: it retains one of two states and therefore stores one bit. The state is retained until an input causes a change. The syllabus includes SR and JK flip-flops. State the circuit convention when interpreting an SR truth table: for a common active-high SR latch made from NOR gates, S=1 sets Q to 1, R=1 resets Q to 0, and S=R=0 holds the previous state. S=R=1 is forbidden for this implementation because it forces both NOR outputs low and violates the complementary-output condition. Other gate implementations may use different active levels; check the diagram before applying this table.</p>
<p>A JK flip-flop removes the SR latch's forbidden input combination: J=0,K=0 holds the current state; J=1,K=0 sets Q=1; J=0,K=1 resets Q=0; J=1,K=1 toggles Q to its complement. Flip-flops provide storage elements in sequential circuits. Distinguish stored state, which depends on previous state, from a combinational gate output, which depends only on current inputs.</p>
<h3>8. Karnaugh maps (K-maps) (15.2)</h3>
<p>A Karnaugh map is a visual way to simplify a Boolean function from its truth table. Place output values in cells labelled by input combinations. Arrange row and column labels in Gray-code order, for example 00, 01, 11, 10, so neighbouring cells differ in exactly one variable. Groups may wrap across opposite edges because those cells are adjacent; diagonal cells are not adjacent.</p>
<p>Group adjacent 1s in rectangles containing a power of two cells (1, 2, 4, 8, and so on). Use the largest useful groups, and overlap groups if that reduces the expression. Each group gives a product term containing only variables that stay constant throughout the group; variables that change are eliminated. OR the terms together. Every 1 must be covered, and do not include a 0 unless the question explicitly provides a don't-care condition. In a three-input map, minterms 1, 3, 5 and 7 are exactly the rows where C=1; they form a group of four and simplify to C. A K-map can make a multi-variable pattern easier to see than repeated algebra, but the final expression must still match every specified row.</p>
<h3>Exam method and common checks</h3><ol><li>Write input labels and output(s) before filling a truth table; for three inputs list all eight combinations exactly once.</li><li>For a circuit, follow wires gate by gate and record intermediate outputs where useful.</li><li>For Boolean simplification, preserve parentheses and complement scope; test the result on a truth table when uncertain.</li><li>For an adder, calculate the binary total and report Sum and Carry separately.</li><li>For a K-map, use Gray-code order, group adjacent 1s in powers of two, and eliminate only variables that change within a group.</li><li>For a control-system scenario, name the sensor input, controller decision, actuator output and (if present) feedback measurement.</li></ol><p><strong>Syllabus basis:</strong> Cambridge International AS &amp; A Level Computer Science 9618 syllabus for 2026, statements 3.1, 3.2 and 15.2; topic boundaries are stated at the start of this note.</p>$pilot$,updated_at=now() where id='a5a4ad94-c42a-4993-87dc-62e555d836b9' and topic_id='532f32af-de30-4ad5-a9be-33e0d552a831' and material_type='notes' and title='Hardware and Logic Gates — Study Notes';
 get diagnostics n=row_count; if n<>1 then raise exception 'Expected one targeted note, updated %',n; end if;
 update public.questions q set question_text=v.new_stem,options=v.opts,correct_option=v.answer_index,explanation=v.explanation,difficulty=v.tier,statement_id=(select st.id from public.syllabus_statements st where st.topic_id='532f32af-de30-4ad5-a9be-33e0d552a831' and st.code=v.code) from (values
($pilot$a988f728-3562-4d9d-8eb5-02a3c756a621$pilot$,$pilot$From the "Hardware and Logic Gates" notes, which statement appears?$pilot$,$pilot$A processor sends a large data block to a slower printer. Which memory use best smooths the transfer?$pilot$,$pilot$["A buffer temporarily queues data until the printer accepts it","ROM stores the print job permanently","The address bus stores the data","Disable the printer queue"]$pilot$::jsonb,0,$pilot$A buffer temporarily queues data between components with different transfer rates, allowing the processor to continue while the slower printer consumes output. It is not permanent storage.$pilot$,$pilot$3.1$pilot$,$pilot$drill$pilot$),
($pilot$0385c7ee-3e20-4d7e-8c91-e32fca34818c$pilot$,$pilot$Which statement belongs to the "Hardware and Logic Gates" notes?$pilot$,$pilot$Which comparison of SRAM and DRAM is accurate?$pilot$,$pilot$["SRAM must be refreshed more often than DRAM","DRAM is faster and more expensive per bit than SRAM","Both lose contents without power; DRAM needs refresh and is denser than SRAM","SRAM is non-volatile, while DRAM is volatile"]$pilot$::jsonb,2,$pilot$Both types are volatile. DRAM stores charge that leaks and therefore requires refresh; its simpler, denser cells make it cheaper per bit, while SRAM is faster and commonly used for cache.$pilot$,$pilot$3.1$pilot$,$pilot$drill$pilot$),
($pilot$29e47e7e-66f6-4c54-b505-add341456ff2$pilot$,$pilot$Which statement is from the "Hardware and Logic Gates" study notes?$pilot$,$pilot$A greenhouse reads a temperature sensor and switches a heater to keep near a target. This is:$pilot$,$pilot$["monitoring only, because it records a reading","open-loop control, because it has an actuator","closed-loop control, because sensor feedback informs heater action","secondary storage, because readings may be saved"]$pilot$::jsonb,2,$pilot$The system measures temperature, compares it with a target and changes heater output. Sensor readings report the effect of that action, forming feedback; monitoring alone would not automatically control the heater.$pilot$,$pilot$3.1$pilot$,$pilot$drill$pilot$),
($pilot$91479ec7-0529-4cdb-bb14-9bd739912e19$pilot$,$pilot$From the "Hardware and Logic Gates" notes, which statement appears?$pilot$,$pilot$A device must retain firmware but allow it to be erased and rewritten electrically while installed. Which memory fits?$pilot$,$pilot$["PROM","EEPROM","SRAM","EPROM erased by ultraviolet light"]$pilot$::jsonb,1,$pilot$EEPROM is electrically erasable and reprogrammable, including in-circuit on suitable devices. PROM is one-time programmable, while EPROM conventionally requires ultraviolet erasure after removal.$pilot$,$pilot$3.1$pilot$,$pilot$drill$pilot$),
($pilot$a21963a2-05d1-4ebf-a3b6-9a2fabbb6d54$pilot$,$pilot$Which of these is covered by the "Hardware and Logic Gates" study notes?$pilot$,$pilot$For a common active-high NOR SR latch, what does S=0 and R=0 do?$pilot$,$pilot$["Sets Q to 1","Resets Q to 0","Forces the forbidden condition","Retains the previous state of Q"]$pilot$::jsonb,3,$pilot$With both active-high inputs at 0, neither NOR gate is commanded to set or reset the latch, so feedback retains the previous state. This assumes the stated NOR implementation and active-high inputs.$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$,$pilot$drill$pilot$)
 ) as v(id,old_stem,new_stem,opts,answer_index,explanation,code,tier) where q.id=v.id::uuid and q.quiz_id='bde4696a-d22a-4751-bc0e-8add89f628a5' and q.question_text=v.old_stem;
 get diagnostics n=row_count; if n<>5 then raise exception 'Expected five exact filler replacements, updated %',n; end if;
 insert into public.questions(quiz_id,question_text,options,correct_option,explanation,sort_order,difficulty,statement_id) select 'bde4696a-d22a-4751-bc0e-8add89f628a5',v.stem,v.opts,v.answer_index,v.explanation,v.position,v.tier,st.id from (values
($pilot$For a two-input NAND gate, A=1 and B=1. What is the output?$pilot$,$pilot$["0","1","A","undefined"]$pilot$::jsonb,0,$pilot$AND(1,1)=1, and NAND complements that result, so the output is 0. NAND is not the same as OR.$pilot$,20,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$3.2$pilot$),
($pilot$A warning output is 1 when sensor A is active and either sensor B or C is active. Which expression matches?$pilot$,$pilot$["A AND (B OR C)","(A AND B) OR C","A OR (B AND C)","NOT A AND (B OR C)"]$pilot$::jsonb,0,$pilot$A must be active in every warning case, while at least one of B or C must also be active. The parentheses make the grouping explicit; alternatives either allow a warning without A or require both B and C.$pilot$,21,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$3.2$pilot$),
($pilot$Evaluate Y=(A OR B) AND NOT C when A=0, B=1, C=1.$pilot$,$pilot$["1","0","A","cannot be determined"]$pilot$::jsonb,1,$pilot$A OR B is 0 OR 1 = 1. NOT C is NOT 1 = 0. The final AND is 1 AND 0 = 0. Evaluate the bracketed operations before the final AND.$pilot$,22,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$3.2$pilot$),
($pilot$Which expression is equivalent to NOT(A OR B)?$pilot$,$pilot$["A OR B","A' OR B'","A' AND B'","A AND B"]$pilot$::jsonb,2,$pilot$By De Morgan's law, complementing an OR changes it to AND and complements each input: (A OR B)' = A' AND B'. This is NOR, not NAND.$pilot$,23,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$Simplify A OR (A AND B).$pilot$,$pilot$["A AND B","A","A OR B","B"]$pilot$::jsonb,1,$pilot$By absorption, A OR (A AND B) = A. If A is 1 the result is 1; if A is 0, A AND B is 0, so the result is 0 regardless of B. Thus B cannot affect the output.$pilot$,24,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$A half adder receives A=1 and B=1. What are its Sum and Carry outputs?$pilot$,$pilot$["Sum=0, Carry=1","Sum=1, Carry=0","Sum=1, Carry=1","Sum=0, Carry=0"]$pilot$::jsonb,0,$pilot$The binary total 1+1 is 10: the least-significant result bit is Sum=0 and the next-column bit is Carry=1. Equivalently XOR gives 0 and AND gives 1; do not swap the outputs.$pilot$,25,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$A full adder receives A=1, B=1 and Cin=0. What are Sum and Cout?$pilot$,$pilot$["Sum=1, Cout=0","Sum=0, Cout=1","Sum=1, Cout=1","Sum=0, Cout=0"]$pilot$::jsonb,1,$pilot$The total is 1+1+0=2, which is binary 10. The current-column sum bit is 0 and carry 1 moves to the next column; a full adder includes the incoming carry.$pilot$,26,$pilot$exam$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$A full adder receives A=1, B=1 and Cin=1. What are Sum and Cout?$pilot$,$pilot$["Sum=1, Cout=1","Sum=0, Cout=1","Sum=1, Cout=0","Sum=0, Cout=0"]$pilot$::jsonb,0,$pilot$The total is 1+1+1=3, or binary 11. The current-column sum bit is 1 and carry-out is 1; carry is generated because at least two inputs are 1, while sum is odd parity.$pilot$,27,$pilot$exam$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$In a Karnaugh map, which cells may be grouped as adjacent?$pilot$,$pilot$["Only diagonal cells","Gray-code labels differing in one bit, including opposite-edge neighbours","Any separated cells containing 1","Only cells in the same row"]$pilot$::jsonb,1,$pilot$K-map adjacency means input combinations differ in exactly one variable. Gray-code ordering makes this visible; opposite edges wrap, but diagonal cells are not adjacent. Group sizes are powers of two.$pilot$,28,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$A 3-input K-map has output 1 for minterms 1,3,5,7, with A most significant and C least significant. Simplify.$pilot$,$pilot$["A'","B'","C","A OR B"]$pilot$::jsonb,2,$pilot$The minterms are 001, 011, 101 and 111. In every row C=1 while A and B vary, so one four-cell group eliminates A and B and leaves C. This group covers all specified 1-cells.$pilot$,29,$pilot$exam$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$For an active-high JK flip-flop, what happens when J=1 and K=1 at the active clock event?$pilot$,$pilot$["Holds current state","Resets to 0","Sets to 1","Toggles to the complement of its current state"]$pilot$::jsonb,3,$pilot$The JK flip-flop's J=K=1 case toggles, unlike the forbidden S=R=1 combination of a common active-high NOR SR latch. The result depends on the clock event, not on continuously changing inputs.$pilot$,30,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$),
($pilot$Which statement correctly distinguishes monitoring from control?$pilot$,$pilot$["Monitoring needs an actuator; control cannot use sensors","Monitoring observes and reports; control can use sensor readings to operate an actuator","Control only stores readings; monitoring changes the process","They are identical terms for any sensor system"]$pilot$::jsonb,1,$pilot$Monitoring observes, records or reports. Control uses the measured state to decide an action and can operate an actuator; feedback can report the result. A sensor alone does not make a system control.$pilot$,31,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$3.1$pilot$),
($pilot$Which memory comparison is correct?$pilot$,$pilot$["PROM is electrically rewritten in-circuit; EEPROM is one-time programmable","EPROM uses periodic refresh; DRAM is non-volatile","PROM is programmed once; EEPROM can be electrically erased and rewritten","SRAM is secondary storage; ROM is volatile"]$pilot$::jsonb,2,$pilot$PROM cannot be erased after one-time programming, whereas EEPROM supports electrical erasure and reprogramming. DRAM refresh and SRAM/ROM volatility are separate distinctions; the alternatives mix unrelated properties.$pilot$,32,$pilot$exam$pilot$,$pilot$3.1$pilot$),
($pilot$A system displays live pressure and automatically opens a valve above a limit. Which description is best?$pilot$,$pilot$["A control system uses the pressure sensor and valve actuator; display is also an output","Monitoring alone opens the valve without control","The valve is input and the sensor is storage","Open-loop system measures only valve position"]$pilot$::jsonb,0,$pilot$The pressure sensor supplies input, the controller checks the threshold, and the valve actuator changes the process. Displaying the reading is monitoring output, but automatic valve action makes the system control; measured pressure can close the loop.$pilot$,33,$pilot$exam$pilot$,$pilot$3.1$pilot$),
($pilot$A truth table is 1 only for A=0,B=1 and A=1,B=0. Which expression matches?$pilot$,$pilot$["A AND B","A OR B","A'B + AB'","A'B'"]$pilot$::jsonb,2,$pilot$The first true row contributes A'B and the second AB'. OR the minterms: A'B + AB'. This is XOR because the inputs differ; the other rows are both 0.$pilot$,34,$pilot$exam$pilot$,$pilot$3.2$pilot$),
($pilot$Which Boolean expression is equivalent to NOT(A AND B)?$pilot$,$pilot$["A' AND B'","A' OR B'","A AND B","A OR B"]$pilot$::jsonb,1,$pilot$De Morgan's law gives (A AND B)' = A' OR B'. Complementing changes AND to OR and complements both inputs; this is NAND, whose output is 0 only when both inputs are 1.$pilot$,35,$pilot$drill$pilot$,$pilot$15.2$pilot$)
 ) as v(stem,opts,answer_index,explanation,position,tier,code) join public.syllabus_statements st on st.topic_id='532f32af-de30-4ad5-a9be-33e0d552a831' and st.code=v.code;
